Credit Application EDA (Home Credit Default Risk)

Exploratory data analysis on a loan application dataset (307,511 rows, 122 columns), covering data cleaning, missing value treatment, and univariate/bivariate/multivariate analysis of factors related to loan application outcomes.

This is a pure EDA project — no predictive model is trained. The goal was to understand the data and surface patterns, not to build a classifier.

## Workflow

### 1. Data Quality & Missing Values

- Dropped columns with more than 47% missing values
- Imputed remaining missing values based on distribution shape and column type:
  - Categorical columns with high missingness (e.g. OCCUPATION_TYPE, ~31% missing) imputed with an explicit "Others" category rather than a measure of central tendency, to avoid distorting the existing class distribution
  - Numerical columns with no significant outliers and mean ≈ median (e.g. EXT_SOURCE_3) imputed with median
  - Columns where 99%+ of values were a single value (e.g. AMT_REQ_CREDIT_BUREAU_*) imputed with mode

### 2. Feature Engineering

- Binned continuous variables (e.g. converting day-based columns to years) for more interpretable grouping

### 3. Imbalance Check

- Assessed class balance across NAME_CONTRACT_STATUS

### 4. Univariate Analysis

- Distribution plots across categorical and numerical columns

### 5. Bivariate & Multivariate Analysis

- Relationships explored include WEEKDAY_APPR_PROCESS_START vs AMT_APPLICATION, and AMT_ANNUITY vs AMT_GOODS_PRICE

### 6. Correlation Analysis

- Correlation among numerical columns computed separately for each loan status segment (Approved, Cancelled, Refused, Unused) — rather than one global correlation, to check whether relationships between variables differ by outcome

### 7. Dataset Merge

- Merged the application dataset with a previous-application dataset for extended analysis

## Requirements

pandas
numpy
matplotlib
seaborn

## Dataset

application_data.csv (not included — Home Credit Default Risk–style loan application data).
